# Functional test: a configured DPD keeps a HEALTHY tunnel up.
# Spec: plan/spec-fixit-ike-test-discrimination.md (item 5, AC-8).
#
# The DPD cleartext defect (plan/learned/1332-fixit-ike-dpd-cleartext.md) sent the
# liveness probe unencrypted. A conforming peer answered nothing, the initiator's
# own timeout fired, and a tunnel that was never broken was torn down. Four unit
# tests in internal/component/ike/engine/rfc7296_dpd_test.go prove the probe is
# BUILT encrypted. None of them runs a peer, so none of them can see the field
# symptom: a configured DPD killing a live SA.
#
# Two unprivileged ze instances negotiate IKEv2 over loopback, exactly as
# ipsec-child-rekey.ci does. Only the INITIATOR carries a short DPD (interval 1,
# timeout 12); the responder's is pinned at the 3600s maximum so it never probes
# and the probe traffic has one direction. maintainSA (engine/established.go)
# ticks once a second, so the initiator raises a probe every second and the
# responder answers each one.
#
# WHAT MAKES THIS DISCRIMINATE, and why every assertion is on STATE:
#   1. `dpd: sent probe`  proves sendDPD (engine/dpd.go) reached the send path.
#   2. `dpd: peer alive`  proves the peer ANSWERED and handleDPDResponse cleared
#      the wait. A probe the peer cannot read never produces this line.
#   3. "uptime-seconds" >= 20 proves ONE SA stayed established across ~20 probe
#      intervals. It is not a sleep: saToMap (ike/cmd/show_ipsec.go) computes it
#      from the SA's own EstablishedAt, and a DPD teardown destroys that SA, so a
#      re-established one starts again at zero. With timeout 12 an unanswered
#      probe ends the SA at ~13s, and every SA after it dies at the same age, so
#      20 is unreachable however long the run takes. A busy host makes the poll
#      take longer; it can never make it pass.
#   4. `dpd: peer dead` is the field symptom itself, rejected by name.
# Both DPD lines are logged at DEBUG by dpd.go, so the ike subsystem is raised.
#
# The 12s DPD timeout is the load margin: the responder may go silent for 12
# seconds before this side calls it dead, and shouldRetransmit repeats the probe
# several times inside that budget, so no single lost datagram ends the tunnel.
#
# MEASURED 2026-08-07, both directions. Blocking BOTH sends in dpd.go -- the one
# in sendDPD and the one in retransmitDPD, which is the shape the cleartext defect
# had, a probe the peer never usefully receives -- turns this RED at step 3:
# "output never matched regexp \"uptime-seconds\":[2-9][0-9] within 40s".
# Restoring both sends turns it GREEN again, 4 suite runs out of 4.
#
# Blocking only sendDPD's send left it GREEN, at a shorter DPD timeout, and that
# is the correct verdict rather than a hole: retransmitDPD still delivers the
# probe, the peer answers it, and the tunnel really is healthy.

option=env:var=ze_test_ike_port:value=$PORT2
# noop dataplane: unprivileged XFRM install is EPERM-fatal by design.
option=env:var=ze_test_ike_dataplane:value=noop
# sendDPD and handleDPDResponse log at DEBUG; the default level is WARN.
option=env:var=ze.log.ike:value=debug
option=timeout:value=60s

# -- Responder daemon (background): binds 127.0.0.2, never probes --
stdin=responder:terminator=EOF_RESPONDER
bgp {
	peer peer1 {
		connection {
			remote {
				ip 127.0.0.1
			}
			local {
				ip 127.0.0.1
				accept false
			}
		}
		session {
			asn {
				local 65533
				remote 65533
			}
			router-id 2.2.2.2
			family {
				ipv4/unicast { prefix { maximum 100; } }
			}
		}
	}
}

vpn {
	ipsec {
		esp-group esp-default {
			lifetime 3600
			proposal 1 {
				encryption aes256
				hash sha256
			}
		}
		ike-group ike-default {
			key-exchange ikev2
			lifetime 28800
			dead-peer-detection {
				interval 3600
				timeout 3600
			}
			proposal 1 {
				dh-group 14
				encryption aes256
				hash sha256
			}
		}
		site-to-site {
			peer initiator {
				ike-group ike-default
				esp-group esp-default
				connection-type respond
				local-address 127.0.0.2
				remote-address 127.0.0.1
				authentication {
					mode pre-shared-secret
					pre-shared-secret test-key-1234
				}
			}
		}
	}
}
EOF_RESPONDER

# -- Initiator daemon (foreground) with the engine-steps executor; SHORT DPD --
stdin=config:terminator=EOF_CONFIG
plugin {
	external engine-steps {
		run "ze-test engine-steps ./engine-steps.json"
		encoder json
	}
}

bgp {
	peer peer1 {
		connection {
			remote {
				ip 127.0.0.1
			}
			local {
				ip 127.0.0.1
				accept false
			}
		}
		session {
			asn {
				local 65533
				remote 65533
			}
			router-id 1.1.1.1
			family {
				ipv4/unicast { prefix { maximum 100; } }
			}
		}
	}
}

vpn {
	ipsec {
		esp-group esp-default {
			lifetime 3600
			proposal 1 {
				encryption aes256
				hash sha256
			}
		}
		ike-group ike-default {
			key-exchange ikev2
			lifetime 28800
			dead-peer-detection {
				action restart
				interval 1
				timeout 12
			}
			proposal 1 {
				dh-group 14
				encryption aes256
				hash sha256
			}
		}
		site-to-site {
			peer peer-1 {
				ike-group ike-default
				esp-group esp-default
				connection-type initiate
				local-address 127.0.0.1
				remote-address 127.0.0.2
				authentication {
					mode pre-shared-secret
					pre-shared-secret test-key-1234
				}
			}
		}
	}
}
EOF_CONFIG

cmd=background:seq=1:exec=ze -:stdin=responder
cmd=foreground:seq=2:exec=ze -:stdin=config:timeout=55s

# Wait for the established SA (aes-cbc appears only after IKE_AUTH), then poll the
# SA's own uptime until it passes 20 seconds. The poll re-dispatches the command;
# it never sleeps, and it reads the daemon's state rather than the test's clock.
command=show vpn ipsec sa | json
expect=output:contains=aes-cbc:timeout=25
expect=output:matches="uptime-seconds":[2-9][0-9]:timeout=40
expect=output:contains="state":"established":timeout=5
expect=exit:code=0
expect=stderr:contains=engine-steps: all steps passed
# The probe left, and the peer answered it. Without the second line the tunnel
# above merely survived a DPD that never ran.
expect=stderr:contains=dpd: sent probe
expect=stderr:contains=dpd: peer alive
# The field symptom of the cleartext defect, named.
reject=stderr:pattern=dpd: peer dead
reject=stderr:pattern=panic recovered
reject=stderr:pattern=fatal error
